<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="ws-artinian-noetherian">
    <title>Exercises related to Artinian and Noetherian modules</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Most of the exercises are taken from <xref ref="bib-Pierce"/> and <xref ref="bib-Jacobson-II"/>. Throughout this worsheet we assume that <m>R</m> is a commutative ring with unity and that <m>A</m> is a right <m>R</m>-algebra. We assume that <m>M</m> is a right <m>A</m>-module.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Show that if <m>A_A</m> is Noetherian (resp., Artinian) then, every finitely-generated right <m>A</m>-module is Noetherian (resp., Artinian). In particular, show that if <m>A</m> is a division ring and <m>M</m> is a finitely-generated right <m>A</m>-module, then <m>M</m> is Noetherian and Artinian.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-endomorphism-artinian-noetherian">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>f\colon M\to M</m> be an <m>A</m>-module endomorphism.
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Show that if <m>M</m> is Artinian then, <m>\Image{f^n}+\ker f^n=M</m> for some positive integer <m>n</m>. Use this to show that <m>f</m> is an isomorphism if and only if it is a monomorphism.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Show that if <m>M</m> is Noetherian then, <m>\Image{f^n}\cap\ker f^n=0</m> for some positive integer <m>n</m>. Use this to show that <m>f</m> is an isomorphism if and only if it is an epimorphism.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise xml:id="ex-Fitting-lemma">
        <title>Fitting's lemma</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>M</m> is a module with a composition series, and <m>f\colon M\to M</m> is an endomorphism, show that there exists a positive integer <m>n</m> such that
                <me>
                    M=\Image{f^n}\oplus\ker f^n.
                </me>
                Furthermore, show that the restriction of <m>f</m> to <m>\Image{f^n}</m> is an automorphism and the restriction of <m>f</m> to <m>\ker f^n</m> is a nilpotent endomorphism.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>M</m> be a right Artinian (resp., Noetherian) <m>A</m>-module. Show that if <m>M^{(r)}\simeq M^{(s)}</m> for some positive integers <m>r,s</m>, then <m>r=s</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Recall that <m>M^{(n)}</m> denotes the direct sum of <m>n</m>-copies of <m>M</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                We may assume that <m>r&lt;s</m> and that <m>M</m> is Noetherian. Consider the projection homomorphism <m>\pi\colon M^{(s)}\to M^{(r)}</m>, and suppose that <m>\varphi\colon M^{(s)}\xrightarrow{\sim}M^{(r)}</m> is the given isomorphism. Consider the endomorphism <m>f=\varphi\circ\pi</m>. As <m>\varphi</m> and <m>\pi</m> are surjective, so is <m>f</m>. Using <xref ref="ex-endomorphism-artinian-noetherian"/>, we conclude that <m>f</m> is an isomorphism. In particular, <m>\pi</m> is an isomorphism onto its image, which is impossible if <m>r&lt;s</m>.
            </p>
        </hint>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>V</m> be a vector space over a field <m>F</m> with a countable basis <m>\{v_1,v_2,\ldots\}</m>. Suppose that <m>T</m> is an <m>F</m>-linear endomorphism of <m>V</m>. Using <m>T</m> we consider <m>V</m> as a right <m>F[t]</m>-module.
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Let <m>T\colon V\to V</m> be defined by <m>T(v_1)=0</m> and <m>T(v_{i+1})=v_i</m> for <m>i=1,2,3,\ldots</m>. Show that <m>F[t]</m>-module <m>V</m> is Artinian. 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Let <m>T\colon V\to V</m> be defined by <m>T(v_i)=v_{i+1}</m> for <m>i=1,2,3,\ldots</m>. Show that <m>F[t]</m>-module <m>V</m> is Noetherian.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>M</m> be a right <m>A</m>-module of finite length <m>\ell(M)</m>, and <m>L,N</m> be submodules of <m>M</m>. Show the following.
                <me>
                    \ell(L+N)+\ell(L\cap N)=\ell(L)+\ell(N)
                </me>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Use the isomorphism <m>(L+N)\big/N\simeq L\big/(L\cap N)</m> and the following short exact sequences.
                <me>
                    0\to N\to L+N\to (L+N)/N\to 0
                </me>
                <me>
                    0\to L\cap N\to L\to L/(L\cap N)
                </me>
                
                
            </p>
        </hint>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>N_1,N_2,\ldots,N_k</m> be submodules of <m>M</m> with each <m>M/N_i</m> has finite length. Show that <m>M/\bigcap_{i=1}^k N_i</m> has finite length.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Determine the length of the <m>\Z</m>-module <m>\Z/{n}\Z</m> for a positive integer <m>n</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Determine for which positive integers <m>n</m>, the module <m>\Z/n\Z</m> has a unique composition series.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Find all compostion series of <m>\Z/12\Z</m>.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Suppose that <m>n=p^k</m> for some prime number <m>p</m> and some positive integer <m>k</m>. Show that the length of the <m>\Z</m>-module <m>\Z_{p^k}</m> is <m>k</m>. For instance,
                            <me>
                                0=p^k\Z/p^k\Z\subsetneq p^{k-1}\Z/p^k\Z\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq p\Z/p^k\Z\subsetneq\Z/p^k\Z
                            </me>
                            is a composition series of <m>\Z_{p^k}</m>.   
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            For a general positive integer <m>n</m>, use the Chinese Remainder Theorem to write <m>\Z_n</m> as a direct sum of <m>\Z_{p_i^{k_i}}</m>'s and use the previous part along with <xref ref="cor-additivity-of-length"/> to conclude.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </hint>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>